'''Amanayé''' (Amanaje) is a possibly extinct [[Tupian languages|Tupi]] language last spoken in the town of São Domingos on the Capim River in [[Pará State]], Brazil.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Steward |first=Julian Haynes |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=mdc_AAAAYAAJ |title=Handbook of South American Indians |date=1946 |publisher=U.S. Government Printing Office |pages=199 |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last1=Derbyshire |first1=Desmond C. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_b5WW9HIwbgC |title=Handbook of Amazonian Languages |last2=Pullum |first2=Geoffrey K. |date=2010-12-14 |publisher=Walter de Gruyter |isbn=978-3-11-082212-0 |volume=1 |pages=495 |language=en}}</ref> The closely related but possibly distinct language is [[Ararandewára language|Ararandewara]], which is spoken at the headwaters of the Moju River.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The lower Amazon : a narrative of explorations in the little known regions of the state of Pará, on the lower Amazon, with a record of archæological excavations on Marajó Island at the mouth of the Amazon River, and observations on the general resources of the county / by Algot Lange ; with an introduction by Frederick S. Dellenbaugh. |url=https://wellcomecollection.org/works/bmehe264 |access-date=2026-01-26 |website=Wellcome Collection |language=en}}</ref> It is unknown whether the Amanayé continue to speak the language or not.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Amanayé - Indigenous Peoples in Brazil |url=https://pib.socioambiental.org/en/Povo:Amanay%C3%A9 |access-date=2025-10-31 |website=pib.socioambiental.org}}</ref>

